Git Commands and Descriptions

1. Command: git --version
   Description: Checks whether Git is installed and shows its version.

2. Command: git config --global user.name "Your Name"
   Description: Sets your Git username.

3. Command: git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
   Description: Sets your Git email address.

4. Command: git init
   Description: Creates a new local Git repository in the current folder.

5. Command: git clone <repository-url>
   Description: Downloads an existing remote repository to your computer.

6. Command: git status
   Description: Shows changed, staged, and untracked files.

7. Command: git add <file-name>
   Description: Adds one file to the staging area.

8. Command: git add .
   Description: Adds all changed files to the staging area.

9. Command: git restore --staged <file-name>
   Description: Removes a file from staging without deleting its changes.

10. Command: git commit -m "message"
    Description: Saves staged changes locally with a message.

11. Command: git log
    Description: Shows commit history.

12. Command: git log --oneline
    Description: Shows short commit history.

13. Command: git diff
    Description: Shows changes that are not yet staged.

14. Command: git diff --staged
    Description: Shows staged changes before committing.

15. Command: git branch
    Description: Lists local branches.

16. Command: git branch <branch-name>
    Description: Creates a new branch.

17. Command: git switch <branch-name>
    Description: Switches to another branch.

18. Command: git switch -c <branch-name>
    Description: Creates and switches to a new branch.

19. Command: git merge <branch-name>
    Description: Merges another branch into the current branch.

20. Command: git branch -d <branch-name>
    Description: Deletes a merged local branch.

21. Command: git remote -v
    Description: Displays connected remote repositories.

22. Command: git remote add origin <repository-url>
    Description: Connects the local repository to GitHub.

23. Command: git push -u origin main
    Description: Uploads the local main branch to GitHub for the first time.

24. Command: git push
    Description: Uploads committed changes to the remote repository.

25. Command: git pull
    Description: Downloads and merges the latest remote changes.

26. Command: git fetch
    Description: Downloads remote changes without merging them.

27. Command: git reset --soft HEAD~1
    Description: Undoes the latest commit but keeps changes staged.

28. Command: git restore <file-name>
    Description: Discards unstaged changes in a file. Use carefully.

29. Command: git stash
    Description: Temporarily saves unfinished changes.

30. Command: git stash pop
    Description: Restores the most recently stashed changes.

31. Command: git tag <tag-name>
    Description: Creates a version tag, such as v1.0.0.
